  {
   "id": "OLmeNPmDBwAJ",
   "from": "Hiroaki Yutani",
   "author": "5e6dd72d433c652e",
   "date": "2017-08-06T14:48:43Z",
   "subject": "Re: Is qplot different from plot?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>plot() is a generic function and knows how to plot because the specific method for stanfit class is implemented in rstan package and the method generates ggplot object (&quot;gg&quot; class) so you can add other layers like geom_line() by + operator.<br><br>qplot() is not such kind of a function; it is just a shortcut for ggplot(). If you need more control over the plot than what plot() automatically does, I guess you will need to execute rstan::extract() and manually compose a ggplot by yourself.<br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">2017-08-06 16:49 GMT+09:00 石浜裕太郎 <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ishiham...@hotmail.com</a>&gt;</span>:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
